1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,476
One man has sold more than six hundred million acres of the Moon.

2
00:00:04,476 --> 00:00:08,300
To more than six million people. In one hundred and eighty countries.

3
00:00:09,433 --> 00:00:14,096
He has never owned a rocket. Never worked for a space agency.

4
00:00:14,096 --> 00:00:18,759
And he started the whole thing broke, divorced, and unemployed, in 1980.

5
00:00:19,899 --> 00:00:24,811
His argument, in his own words: I filed my claim of ownership as an individual.

6
00:00:24,811 --> 00:00:29,068
And forty five years later, nobody has ever proven him wrong in court.

7
00:00:30,199 --> 00:00:32,533
So is Dennis Hope a con man? A visionary?

8
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:37,201
Or did he really find a hole in international law big enough to fly the whole Moon through?

9
00:00:38,333 --> 00:00:45,020
This is the true story of the most successful real estate deal that has never actually delivered a single square foot of real estate.

10
00:00:46,166 --> 00:00:47,655
You're watching mindwired.

11
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:50,367
The Man Who Sold the Moon.

12
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:54,582
Chapter one. A broke man looks at the sky.

13
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:58,740
Nineteen eighty. Dennis Hope is a small-time American salesman.

14
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:02,455
Some accounts say a car lot. Some say he did ventriloquism.

15
00:01:02,455 --> 00:01:05,496
What everyone agrees on is this: he was broke.

16
00:01:06,633 --> 00:01:10,985
He was going through a divorce. By his own account, unemployed for almost a year.

17
00:01:10,985 --> 00:01:14,757
And one night, looking out his window, he looked up at the Moon.

18
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:19,907
And he had a thought that sounds almost too simple to be real. Hey.

19
00:01:19,907 --> 00:01:23,057
There's a load of property up there. And nobody owns it.

20
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:27,376
It is worth pausing here. This was not a stupid man.

21
00:01:27,376 --> 00:01:34,884
He was a salesman, and like every good salesman, he had just found the one gap in the rulebook nobody else had bothered to read closely.

22
00:01:36,033 --> 00:01:40,886
He wasn't wrong about one thing. There is a real treaty that governs who can own the Moon.

23
00:01:40,886 --> 00:01:43,582
And Hope believed he had found a gap in it.

24
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:49,108
In 1967, the world's spacefaring nations signed the Outer Space Treaty.

25
00:01:49,108 --> 00:01:59,929
Article Two says the Moon is, quote, not subject to national appropriation by claim of sovereignty, by means of use or occupation, or by any other means.

26
00:02:01,066 --> 00:02:04,261
That treaty did not come from nowhere. It came from fear.

27
00:02:04,261 --> 00:02:13,265
In the nineteen sixties, both the United States and the Soviet Union were racing to the Moon, and neither wanted the other to plant a flag and call it a colony.

28
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:20,146
So the world's major powers agreed, in advance, that nobody would be allowed to claim it.

29
00:02:20,146 --> 00:02:28,767
Two years later, in nineteen sixty nine, Apollo eleven landed, and planted an American flag on ground that, by treaty, no nation could own.

30
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:33,308
Read that closely. It says nations cannot own the Moon.

31
00:02:33,308 --> 00:02:37,057
Dennis Hope noticed something else. It never says the word individual.

32
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:48,153
So in 1980, Hope filed a written declaration claiming ownership of the Moon, and every other planet in the solar system except Earth, and its moons, in his own name.

33
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:52,623
He mailed notice of his claim to the United Nations.

34
00:02:52,623 --> 00:02:55,947
And, by his account, to the American and Soviet governments.

35
00:02:55,947 --> 00:02:58,939
Then he waited for someone to tell him no.

36
00:03:00,066 --> 00:03:04,326
Nobody ever did. No government has ever formally challenged his claim.

37
00:03:04,326 --> 00:03:09,748
Not once, in forty five years. Hope calls that silent consent. It is not.

38
00:03:09,748 --> 00:03:15,557
Silence does not create a title. It just means nobody bothered to answer a letter.

39
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:23,482
There was actually a second treaty, in 1979, that tried to close this exact gap for good.

40
00:03:23,482 --> 00:03:29,866
It said no state, no organization, and no, quote, natural person could own the Moon either.

41
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,785
But almost no spacefaring nation ever signed it. Not the United States. Not Russia.

42
00:03:35,785 --> 00:03:36,469
Not China.

43
00:03:36,469 --> 00:03:42,964
Without the major powers on board, the treaty that would have definitively stopped Hope never really had any teeth.

44
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:46,529
Chapter two. Twenty dollars an acre.

45
00:03:47,666 --> 00:03:51,622
Hope founded something called the Lunar Embassy. And started selling the Moon.

46
00:03:51,622 --> 00:03:54,588
One acre at a time. For about twenty dollars.

47
00:03:55,733 --> 00:03:58,119
That twenty dollar price held for decades.

48
00:03:58,119 --> 00:04:02,892
Today, on his own website, the same acre costs closer to thirty five dollars.

49
00:04:02,892 --> 00:04:04,596
Prime real estate costs more.

50
00:04:04,596 --> 00:04:09,369
A plot with a view of where a spacecraft actually landed runs about fifty.

51
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:16,273
What do you actually get? A parchment deed with gold trim. A map with your acre circled.

52
00:04:16,273 --> 00:04:20,688
A little booklet. And, later, a copy of his own written lunar constitution.

53
00:04:21,833 --> 00:04:27,287
By his own count, Hope says he has sold more than six hundred and eleven million acres.

54
00:04:27,287 --> 00:04:31,779
To more than six million customers. In more than one hundred and eighty countries.

55
00:04:31,779 --> 00:04:34,346
For around eleven million dollars over four decades.

56
00:04:35,466 --> 00:04:40,420
Do the math on that acreage. The Moon has about nine point four billion acres total.

57
00:04:40,420 --> 00:04:45,993
Hope's own claimed sales add up to something like six or seven percent of the entire lunar surface.

58
00:04:47,133 --> 00:04:51,296
None of those numbers are audited. There are no independent financial records.

59
00:04:51,296 --> 00:04:55,806
Every figure in this chapter is Hope's own count, not a verified fact.

60
00:04:56,933 --> 00:04:59,404
And the marketing gets bigger from there.

61
00:04:59,404 --> 00:05:06,112
Hope has claimed, over the years, that three former US presidents own lunar deeds, bought as gifts by aides.

62
00:05:06,112 --> 00:05:08,584
And that Hollywood stars are customers too.

63
00:05:09,733 --> 00:05:14,566
Not one of those names has ever confirmed it. Not one president's office.

64
00:05:14,566 --> 00:05:15,682
Not one celebrity.

65
00:05:15,682 --> 00:05:20,887
The most careful reporting on this story has never named a single verified buyer.

66
00:05:22,033 --> 00:05:26,912
For an extra fee, buyers can also purchase the mineral rights beneath their acre.

67
00:05:26,912 --> 00:05:34,232
Whatever gold, whatever rare metal, whatever ice sits under that patch of lunar dust, technically, on paper, it's now yours too.

68
00:05:35,366 --> 00:05:39,251
Nobody has ever mined a single gram of it. Nobody has any way to.

69
00:05:39,251 --> 00:05:45,632
It is one more layer of paper, wrapped around a piece of paper, describing a place none of the buyers will ever visit.

70
00:05:46,766 --> 00:05:52,304
In 2004, Hope went further. He founded something he calls the Galactic Government.

71
00:05:52,304 --> 00:05:57,842
With a congress. A patent office. And its own currency, called the Delta.

72
00:05:58,966 --> 00:06:05,480
He has said the Delta is backed by lunar helium three, worth an estimated six quadrillion dollars.

73
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:09,311
No nation recognizes this government. No bank recognizes this currency.

74
00:06:09,311 --> 00:06:12,759
It exists entirely on paper, and on his website.

75
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:19,506
He has even asked the International Monetary Fund to formally recognize the Delta as real currency.

76
00:06:19,506 --> 00:06:20,908
So far, it hasn't.

77
00:06:20,908 --> 00:06:28,267
A currency backed by a metal nobody has ever mined, issued by a government no country accepts, spent nowhere on Earth.

78
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:34,463
And here is the strangest part of the whole business. Hope has never really hidden.

79
00:06:34,463 --> 00:06:40,540
He has spent over four decades giving interviews, to major newspapers, to television crews, to anyone who asks.

80
00:06:40,540 --> 00:06:42,903
He is not running from this story.

81
00:06:42,903 --> 00:06:46,954
He is telling it, over and over, to anyone who will listen.

82
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:50,686
Chapter three. The scholars disagree.

83
00:06:51,833 --> 00:06:57,780
So does the loophole actually work? We asked the people who study space law for a living.

84
00:06:57,780 --> 00:06:59,879
And the answer, overwhelmingly, is no.

85
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,367
Ram Jakhu, of the McGill Institute of Air and Space Law, put it plainly.

86
00:07:05,367 --> 00:07:10,359
The Outer Space Treaty is public law, and as such, private citizens are subject to it.

87
00:07:10,359 --> 00:07:11,919
I don't see a loophole.

88
00:07:13,066 --> 00:07:21,350
The president of the International Institute of Space Law, Tanja Masson-Zwaan, said the treaty applies to governments and their private citizens alike.

89
00:07:21,350 --> 00:07:23,985
Which invalidates Hope's claim to the Moon.

90
00:07:25,133 --> 00:07:26,364
Their reasoning is simple.

91
00:07:26,364 --> 00:07:30,363
A government cannot grant a right that it itself is forbidden to hold.

92
00:07:30,363 --> 00:07:36,209
If a nation cannot own the Moon, it cannot hand that ownership to one of its own citizens either.

93
00:07:37,333 --> 00:07:39,906
To be fair, it is not unanimous.

94
00:07:39,906 --> 00:07:48,362
A minority of legal scholars have argued the treaty's text really does only bind nations, leaving a genuine gray area for private claims.

95
00:07:48,362 --> 00:07:52,406
This is a real, ongoing legal debate. It is not settled.

96
00:07:53,533 --> 00:08:01,259
There have reportedly been fraud complaints filed against Lunar Embassy operations in Germany, in Sweden, and in the Netherlands.

97
00:08:01,259 --> 00:08:05,732
Every single one was reportedly dismissed, for the same strange reason.

98
00:08:06,866 --> 00:08:10,314
No court in Europe would rule the Moon fell inside its own jurisdiction.

99
00:08:10,314 --> 00:08:15,617
If you cannot even agree the Moon is somewhere a court can reach, you cannot rule on who owns it.

100
00:08:16,766 --> 00:08:21,364
There is a specific mechanism behind that reasoning, and it has a name.

101
00:08:21,364 --> 00:08:23,487
Article Six of the same treaty.

102
00:08:23,487 --> 00:08:29,853
It says every government is responsible for authorizing and supervising the space activities of its own private citizens.

103
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:37,099
In plain language, a citizen doesn't get to do in space what their own government is banned from doing.

104
00:08:37,099 --> 00:08:39,346
The United States cannot own the Moon.

105
00:08:39,346 --> 00:08:43,199
So, this argument goes, neither can one of its citizens acting alone.

106
00:08:44,333 --> 00:08:47,759
So has anyone ever actually taken Hope to court over this? Almost.

107
00:08:47,759 --> 00:08:52,614
And the one time it happened, it did not go well for the company selling the Moon.

108
00:08:53,733 --> 00:08:56,659
Chapter four. The one time a court ruled.

109
00:08:57,800 --> 00:08:59,266
Two thousand five. Beijing.

110
00:08:59,266 --> 00:09:08,066
A company calling itself Hope's official partner in China had sold forty nine acres of the Moon, to thirty four customers, in three days.

111
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:16,075
Chinese regulators suspended the company's business license within days, for what they called speculation and profiteering.

112
00:09:16,075 --> 00:09:17,793
The certificates were seized.

113
00:09:18,933 --> 00:09:20,855
The company appealed. And lost.

114
00:09:20,855 --> 00:09:29,695
A Beijing court ruled that no individual, and no state, can own the Moon — citing the same nineteen sixty seven treaty, directly.

115
00:09:30,833 --> 00:09:36,968
It is the single, clearest piece of case law that exists anywhere on this entire industry.

116
00:09:36,968 --> 00:09:42,719
A real court, actually applying the real treaty, to a real company selling the Moon.

117
00:09:43,866 --> 00:09:47,886
That is the single clearest court ruling anywhere on this entire industry.

118
00:09:47,886 --> 00:09:52,240
But be precise about what it was. It was against a Chinese affiliate.

119
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:55,255
Not against Dennis Hope, personally, in the United States.

120
00:09:56,399 --> 00:10:02,779
In fact, no court, anywhere in the world, has ever ruled directly on whether Dennis Hope personally owns the Moon.

121
00:10:02,779 --> 00:10:05,969
And no court has ever found him guilty of fraud.

122
00:10:05,969 --> 00:10:08,521
He has never been criminally charged with anything.

123
00:10:09,666 --> 00:10:13,990
Critics have called his business a hollow claim. A fraud. Even a joke.

124
00:10:13,990 --> 00:10:16,318
Those are their words. Not a verdict.

125
00:10:16,318 --> 00:10:23,302
Hope has spent forty five years selling the exact same story, and no legal system on Earth has ever stopped him.

126
00:10:24,433 --> 00:10:27,428
You may have heard a different version of this story.

127
00:10:27,428 --> 00:10:31,023
That an American court finally ruled Dennis Hope doesn't own the Moon.

128
00:10:31,023 --> 00:10:33,419
That version is wrong, and it's worth correcting.

129
00:10:34,566 --> 00:10:39,108
In 2020, there was a real US federal case with Hope's name on it.

130
00:10:39,108 --> 00:10:43,000
But he wasn't the one being sued. He was the one suing.

131
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:48,515
A rival called Lunarlandowner dot com, over who had the right to use the name Lunar Embassy.

132
00:10:49,633 --> 00:10:52,200
It was a fight over a brand name.

133
00:10:52,200 --> 00:10:57,012
Nobody in that courtroom ever ruled on whether the Moon itself could legally be owned.

134
00:10:57,012 --> 00:11:02,146
Get the story right: the only real court ruling on ownership came from Beijing, not America.

135
00:11:03,266 --> 00:11:05,225
So who actually buys a piece of the Moon?

136
00:11:06,366 --> 00:11:09,786
Six million transactions is a lot of people to generalize about.

137
00:11:09,786 --> 00:11:15,692
But the pattern in how these deeds actually get used is remarkably consistent, once you look past the marketing.

138
00:11:16,833 --> 00:11:19,765
It's a wedding present for a couple who love astronomy.

139
00:11:19,765 --> 00:11:25,630
A birthday gift for a father who watched the Moon landing live, as a kid, and never stopped looking up.

140
00:11:25,630 --> 00:11:28,562
A gag gift, wrapped and handed over with a laugh.

141
00:11:29,700 --> 00:11:31,861
Almost nobody frames it as an investment.

142
00:11:31,861 --> 00:11:35,257
Nobody expects to sell their acre for a profit one day.

143
00:11:35,257 --> 00:11:38,036
The twenty dollar price point is not an accident.

144
00:11:38,036 --> 00:11:41,741
It is deliberately cheap enough that losing the bet costs you nothing.

145
00:11:42,866 --> 00:11:49,426
And that, more than any legal argument, may be the real secret of why this business has survived forty five years.

146
00:11:49,426 --> 00:11:51,300
It was never really selling land.

147
00:11:51,300 --> 00:11:57,547
It was selling a feeling, at a price low enough that nobody ever felt cheated enough to fight back hard.

148
00:11:58,666 --> 00:12:01,095
Chapter six. The man himself.

149
00:12:02,233 --> 00:12:06,941
Every treaty, every court case, every scholar we've quoted, points the same direction.

150
00:12:06,941 --> 00:12:10,200
So why hasn't this ever actually been shut down?

151
00:12:11,333 --> 00:12:15,858
So who is Dennis Hope, really? Because he is still out there.

152
00:12:15,858 --> 00:12:20,006
Still running the Lunar Embassy. Still, by every account, completely sincere.

153
00:12:21,133 --> 00:12:24,150
When scholars dismiss his claim, Hope's response is simple.

154
00:12:24,150 --> 00:12:29,179
That has no credence, he has said. We don't need the recognition of the world.

155
00:12:30,299 --> 00:12:31,704
He points to history.

156
00:12:31,704 --> 00:12:39,781
The American frontier was claimed the same way, he argues, one homestead at a time, long before any government formally recognized the border.

157
00:12:40,899 --> 00:12:44,657
He is thinking of the Homestead Act of eighteen sixty two.

158
00:12:44,657 --> 00:12:51,147
A settler could claim empty land, live on it, work it, and after five years, it was legally theirs.

159
00:12:51,147 --> 00:12:54,221
No spaceship required. Just paperwork, and nobody stopping you.

160
00:12:55,366 --> 00:12:59,495
It is a clever comparison. It is also not the same thing.

161
00:12:59,495 --> 00:13:01,215
Homesteaders lived on their land.

162
00:13:01,215 --> 00:13:05,688
Hope has never set foot on a single acre he claims to own.

163
00:13:05,688 --> 00:13:09,472
Nobody ever will, at twenty dollars an acre, any time soon.

164
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:12,559
And he points to his customers.

165
00:13:12,559 --> 00:13:18,110
By his own count, in thirty two years, only six people ever asked him for a refund.

166
00:13:18,110 --> 00:13:20,396
Out of more than six million buyers.

167
00:13:21,533 --> 00:13:25,449
Here is the honest, unresolved question at the center of this whole story.

168
00:13:25,449 --> 00:13:29,366
Is Dennis Hope a sincere believer who genuinely thinks he owns the Moon?

169
00:13:29,366 --> 00:13:35,090
Or a showman who has spent forty five years selling a piece of paper he always knew was worthless?

170
00:13:36,233 --> 00:13:38,669
Because here is the part almost nobody talks about.

171
00:13:38,669 --> 00:13:42,189
Most of his customers never thought they were buying real estate at all.

172
00:13:43,333 --> 00:13:47,162
A wedding gift. A birthday joke for the uncle obsessed with space.

173
00:13:47,162 --> 00:13:50,672
A stocking stuffer nobody expected to hold up in a courtroom.

174
00:13:50,672 --> 00:13:55,140
For twenty dollars, almost nobody actually believed they owned a piece of the Moon.

175
00:13:56,266 --> 00:13:59,300
No court has answered that. No government has answered it.

176
00:13:59,300 --> 00:14:05,670
Millions of people have simply decided they don't need an answer to enjoy owning a tiny, imaginary piece of the sky.

177
00:14:06,799 --> 00:14:08,758
Chapter seven. Not the only one.

178
00:14:09,899 --> 00:14:19,616
There are competing lunar registries today, some older than the internet itself, each claiming to be the real deal, each selling certificates that look almost identical to Hope's.

179
00:14:20,766 --> 00:14:23,578
Here is the part that should really give you pause.

180
00:14:23,578 --> 00:14:26,670
Dennis Hope did not stay the only person selling the Moon.

181
00:14:26,670 --> 00:14:31,450
Because it turns out, once you show the world a business model works, other people copy it.

182
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:43,989
Rival lunar-land companies have popped up selling the same acres, using the same treaty argument, sometimes even claiming to be his official partner, the way the Chinese company did before its license was pulled.

183
00:14:45,133 --> 00:14:48,854
There is no registry that can settle any of these disputes.

184
00:14:48,854 --> 00:14:52,576
No government office keeps track of who supposedly owns which crater.

185
00:14:52,576 --> 00:14:59,004
Multiple people can, and do, sell the exact same acre to different buyers, and nothing on Earth stops them.

186
00:15:00,133 --> 00:15:05,484
That, more than any court ruling, might be the clearest proof of what these deeds actually are.

187
00:15:05,484 --> 00:15:06,114
Not property.

188
00:15:06,114 --> 00:15:12,410
Just a story, printed on nice paper, that as many people as want to can tell at the same time.

189
00:15:13,533 --> 00:15:17,060
So what stays with me about this story isn't the legal argument.

190
00:15:17,060 --> 00:15:19,411
It's not even the six hundred million acres.

191
00:15:20,533 --> 00:15:29,371
It's that a man who owned nothing, in 1980, looked up at the one thing every human being on Earth can see at once, and decided it was his.

192
00:15:29,371 --> 00:15:34,247
And forty five years later, millions of people still want to believe he might be right.

193
00:15:35,366 --> 00:15:39,863
There is something almost sweet buried inside a story this absurd.

194
00:15:39,863 --> 00:15:47,631
Every single night, for all of human history, people have looked up at that same Moon and felt something.

195
00:15:47,631 --> 00:15:51,719
Wonder. Loneliness. Hope, in the ordinary sense of that word.

196
00:15:52,866 --> 00:15:59,306
Dennis Hope just found a way to sell that feeling back to people, on a piece of paper, for twenty dollars.

197
00:15:59,306 --> 00:16:03,906
That may be the most honest way to describe his entire business. Not real estate.

198
00:16:03,906 --> 00:16:06,972
A receipt for a feeling everybody already had for free.

199
00:16:08,100 --> 00:16:16,023
If one man claiming the Moon sounds strange, wait until you see a company that convinced two hundred thousand people to sign up for a one way trip there.

200
00:16:16,023 --> 00:16:18,208
Watch our film on the Mars One scam.

201
00:16:19,333 --> 00:16:21,307
This has been the true story of the man who sold the Moon.

202
00:16:21,307 --> 00:16:25,408
If a claim this strange could sell to millions, wait until you see the one that sold two hundred thousand people a ticket to actually die there.

203
00:16:25,408 --> 00:16:25,864
Subscribe to mindwired.

